A||||9||GT Bastard||How much does a fiero gas tank weigh??||||5||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||11-27-2000||08:13 AM||||If anyone has an answer, please fill me in!  Thanks...||||reg||5
Z||000001||Shaun41178||11-27-2000||10:06 AM||||without any gas in it ist is really light.  i would say it is around 10-15 pounds.  Maybe 20 pounds. More bulky then heavy.  it isn't hard to drop from the car when empty and can be handled by one person as it isn't that heavy.  hope this helps<P>------------------<BR><BR><A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>www.ILOVESPEEDO.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||11-27-2000||01:41 PM||||I'm contemplating the use of a fuel cell(the plastic circle track type summit cells) to replace the gas tank.  I'm removing the spare tire holder for the fuel cell and battery to help even-out the weight, free up some space in the engine bay, and maybe help with the front end push. <P>thanks for the info shaun!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Alex4mula||11-27-2000||02:42 PM||||Theory sounds interesting but also think about safety issues. I'm not too familiar with fuel cells but having fuel in front of me close to an impact zone doesn't make me feel safe. Also what about the weight of 5 to 12 gallons of fuel switching from side to side while cornering? What effect could that inertia way up front have in the Fiero handling? Just for you to consider.||||reg||1
Z||000004||PJ||11-27-2000||05:08 PM||||I have one laying around here so I weighed it. Fourteen pounds without the pump or sender.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||11-27-2000||07:08 PM||||I would tend to agree Alex about the weight of that fuel switching sides in a corner. Could create worse cornering then before.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Spyder||11-27-2000||08:41 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.markalexandermiller.com/gastank.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.markalexandermiller.com/frntcompart.jpg"> <BR>I installed a 5 gallon auxiliary tank in the front. The sunroof still fits in the stock location.<BR>The battery can be moved more forward than where I have it and you can easily mount at least a 10 gallon tank. Also there is a lot more room below where I mounted the fuel cell.  As far as sloshing, it has fuel foam inside.<BR>If I remember right, didn’t the Porsche 911 have the gas tank in the front nose.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||11-27-2000||09:33 PM||||well..... moving the battery is pretty well established to help. Unless you are racing constantly I have to wonder at the logic of moving the fuel tank. I think if you have a car that's raced and driven on the street then a better idea would be some type of removable dead weight up front. Adding to much weight up front could cause a bottoming problem. Do that to much and things, like ball joints, start to break.<P>At the very least I'd experiment with removable weight before moving the tank. Try throwing diferant amounts of sand bags up there and see what that does for you. I do that in winter to stick the front a little more but I wouldn't want the weight in all the time. But then mine is also only a 4 cyl 87 SC and the weight setup is pretty good to start with.||||reg||1
Z||000008||robertjay||11-27-2000||10:14 PM||||Plus the fuel cells used in race cars are filled with foam, so I don't thing sloshing fuel side to side is a problem.||||reg||1
Z||000009||GT Bastard||11-27-2000||10:47 PM||||I'm not worried about the sloshing issue at all.  There just isn't enough fuel there to cause a problem.  ||||reg||1
